COURSE.push({
id: 'm5', short: 'Assertions', title: 'Assertions: checking the result', minutes: 55,
lead: 'How to prove that the page did the right thing, why the check must be able to wait, and the one habit that makes most flaky tests.',
learn: ['write web-first assertions on a locator and on a page', 'explain why a plain assert on a value read once is a trap', 'choose the right assertion for a situation', 'set a different timeout for one slow check'],
body: `
<h2>A test without a check proves nothing</h2>
<p>A test that clicks through a page and never checks anything passes even when the page is broken. An <b>assertion</b> is the sentence in your test that says "this must be true". If it is not true, the test fails and tells you why. Every test should end with at least one assertion that matches the purpose of the test.</p>
<h2>A photo or a video</h2>
<p>There are two ways to check that "Report ready" is on the page. A <b>plain</b> check reads the page <b>once</b> and compares, like taking a photo. If the page needs another half second, the photo shows the old state and the test fails, although the page is fine. A <b>web-first</b> assertion is like a <b>video</b>: Playwright looks again and again, for up to 5 seconds by default, until the condition is true. It fails only if the condition never becomes true in that time.</p>
${tbl(['', 'Web-first assertion', 'Plain assertion on a value read once'], [
  ['Python', '<code>expect(locator).to_be_visible()</code>', '<code>assert locator.is_visible()</code>'],
  ['Kotlin', '<code>assertThat(locator).isVisible()</code>', '<code>assertTrue(locator.isVisible())</code>'],
  ['Behaviour', 'Retries until true or the timeout', 'Answers at once, and never retries'],
  ['On a slow page', 'Passes', 'Fails sometimes: flaky']
])}
${co('The habit to build', 'Always assert with <code>expect(...)</code> (Python) or <code>assertThat(...)</code> (Kotlin) on a <b>locator or a page</b>. Avoid reading a value first (<code>inner_text()</code>, <code>is_visible()</code>, <code>count()</code>) and then comparing it with a plain assert. Those read once and cannot wait.', true)}
{{watch:s5}}
<h2>The assertions you will use most</h2>
${tbl(['Check', 'Python', 'Kotlin'], [
  ['The element is on the page and visible', '<code>to_be_visible()</code>', '<code>isVisible()</code>'],
  ['The element is gone or not shown', '<code>to_be_hidden()</code>', '<code>isHidden()</code>'],
  ['The whole text is exactly this', '<code>to_have_text("Buy milk")</code>', '<code>hasText("Buy milk")</code>'],
  ['The text contains this part', '<code>to_contain_text("milk")</code>', '<code>containsText("milk")</code>'],
  ['An input has this value', '<code>to_have_value("ada")</code>', '<code>hasValue("ada")</code>'],
  ['How many elements match', '<code>to_have_count(3)</code>', '<code>hasCount(3)</code>'],
  ['A checkbox is ticked', '<code>to_be_checked()</code>', '<code>isChecked()</code>'],
  ['A control is enabled or disabled', '<code>to_be_enabled()</code>, <code>to_be_disabled()</code>', '<code>isEnabled()</code>, <code>isDisabled()</code>'],
  ['The address of the page (a page assertion)', '<code>expect(page).to_have_url(...)</code>', '<code>assertThat(page).hasURL(...)</code>'],
  ['The title of the page (a page assertion)', '<code>expect(page).to_have_title(...)</code>', '<code>assertThat(page).hasTitle(...)</code>'],
  ['The opposite of any check', '<code>not_to_be_visible()</code>, <code>not_to_have_text(...)</code>', '<code>assertThat(locator).not().isVisible()</code>']
])}
{{w:assertpick}}
<p>Two details. <b>to_have_text</b> compares the <b>whole</b> text (extra spaces are ignored), while <b>to_contain_text</b> needs only a part. And for addresses and titles you can pass a regular expression instead of a string when only a part matters (<code>re.compile("/todomvc/")</code> in Python, <code>Pattern.compile("/todomvc/")</code> in Kotlin).</p>
<h2>Timeouts</h2>
<p>A web-first assertion waits up to <b>5 seconds</b>. That is enough for almost everything, and when it is not, resist the urge to raise it for the whole project. Raise it for the one slow check, so that real problems elsewhere still show up quickly.</p>
${code({ py: 'expect(page.get_by_text("Report ready")).to_be_visible(timeout=15_000)\n\n# or for the whole test run, in conftest.py\nexpect.set_options(timeout=10_000)', kt: 'assertThat(page.getByText("Report ready"))\n    .isVisible(LocatorAssertions.IsVisibleOptions().setTimeout(15_000.0))\n\n// or for the whole test run, for example in BaseTest\nPlaywrightAssertions.setDefaultAssertionTimeout(10_000.0)', file: { py: 'Timeouts', kt: 'Timeouts' } })}
<h2>Practise in the lab</h2>
{{lab:l5a}}
{{lab:l5b}}
<h2>Step by step: assertions on a real page</h2>
${steps('m5', [
  { do: 'Add the assertions test to your project.', code: code({ py: 'import re\n\nfrom playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_assertions_on_the_todo_list(page: Page):\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    new_todo = page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?")\n    new_todo.fill("Buy milk")\n    new_todo.press("Enter")\n    new_todo.fill("Walk the dog")\n    new_todo.press("Enter")\n\n    items = page.get_by_test_id("todo-item")\n    expect(items).to_have_count(2)\n    expect(items.first).to_contain_text("milk")\n    expect(items.nth(1)).to_have_text("Walk the dog")\n    expect(new_todo).to_be_empty()\n    expect(page.get_by_role("button", name="Clear completed")).to_be_hidden()\n\n    items.first.get_by_role("checkbox").check()\n    expect(items.first.get_by_role("checkbox")).to_be_checked()\n    expect(page.get_by_role("button", name="Clear completed")).to_be_visible()\n\n    expect(page).to_have_url(re.compile("/todomvc/"))\n    expect(page).to_have_title(re.compile("TodoMVC"))', kt: 'package course\n\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.Page\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.assertions.PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.options.AriaRole\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test\nimport java.util.regex.Pattern\n\nclass AssertionsTest : BaseTest() {\n    @Test\n    fun assertionsOnTheTodoList() {\n        page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n        val newTodo = page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")\n        newTodo.fill("Buy milk")\n        newTodo.press("Enter")\n        newTodo.fill("Walk the dog")\n        newTodo.press("Enter")\n\n        val items = page.getByTestId("todo-item")\n        val clearCompleted = page.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON, Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Clear completed"))\n        assertThat(items).hasCount(2)\n        assertThat(items.first()).containsText("milk")\n        assertThat(items.nth(1)).hasText("Walk the dog")\n        assertThat(newTodo).isEmpty()\n        assertThat(clearCompleted).isHidden()\n\n        items.first().getByRole(AriaRole.CHECKBOX).check()\n        assertThat(items.first().getByRole(AriaRole.CHECKBOX)).isChecked()\n        assertThat(clearCompleted).isVisible()\n\n        assertThat(page).hasURL(Pattern.compile("/todomvc/"))\n        assertThat(page).hasTitle(Pattern.compile("TodoMVC"))\n    }\n}', file: { py: 'tests/test_assertions.py', kt: 'src/test/kotlin/course/AssertionsTest.kt' }, ex: 'assertions' }), why: 'Read each assertion as a sentence: "the items must have a count of 2", "the first item must contain milk", "the Clear completed button must be hidden". The button appears only after a todo is ticked, which is why the same assertion comes twice with different outcomes.', see: 'Nothing yet, the file only exists.' },
  { do: 'Run it.', code: sh({ py: 'pytest tests/test_assertions.py', kt: './gradlew test --tests "course.AssertionsTest"' }), why: 'A green run first, so that every later change is clearly yours.', see: L('<code>1 passed</code>', '<code>AssertionsTest > assertionsOnTheTodoList() PASSED</code>') },
  { do: 'Change one expected value on purpose, for example <code>to_have_count(3)</code> instead of 2, and run again. Read the failure.', why: 'You should know what a failing web-first assertion looks like before you meet it in real work: it prints what it expected, what it received, and a call log of its retries.', see: 'The test fails after about 5 seconds, because the assertion kept trying until its timeout. Then change it back.' },
  { do: 'Make the timeout visible: set a short one on that same wrong assertion and run again.', code: code({ py: 'expect(items).to_have_count(3, timeout=1000)', kt: 'assertThat(items).hasCount(3, LocatorAssertions.HasCountOptions().setTimeout(1000.0))', label: 'A one-second timeout (add the import for LocatorAssertions in Kotlin)' }), why: 'A short timeout makes a failing test fail fast, which is nice while you debug. You also see that the 5 seconds were the assertion retrying, not a slow test.', see: 'The failure now comes after about one second. Change both lines back.' }
])}
`,
takeaways: ['An assertion states what must be true. A test without one proves nothing.', 'A web-first assertion retries until the condition is true or the timeout ends. A plain assert on a value read once cannot wait.', 'Assert on a locator or a page: to_be_visible, to_have_text, to_have_count, to_have_url, and their opposites.', 'The default timeout is 5 seconds. Raise it on the one slow check, not for everything.', 'Most flaky tests are plain checks, or fixed sleeps, in disguise.'],
quiz: [
  { q: 'What makes an assertion "web-first"?', o: ['It retries until the condition is true or the timeout ends', 'It runs before the page loads', 'It only works on the first page of a test', 'It checks the web server, not the page'], a: 0, why: 'Retrying is the whole idea. It lets the test cope with a page that needs a moment.' },
  { q: 'Which check is the most reliable way to see that "Done" appears?', o: [L('expect(page.get_by_text("Done")).to_be_visible()', 'assertThat(page.getByText("Done")).isVisible()'), L('assert page.get_by_text("Done").is_visible()', 'assertTrue(page.getByText("Done").isVisible())'), L('assert page.get_by_text("Done").count() == 1', 'assertEquals(1, page.getByText("Done").count())'), L('time.sleep(2) and then a plain assert', 'Thread.sleep(2000) and then assertTrue(...)')], a: 0, why: 'Only the first one retries. The others read the page once, or guess a delay.' },
  { type: 'num', q: 'How many seconds does a web-first assertion wait by default?', ans: 5, tol: 0.01, why: 'The default is 5 seconds. Actions wait up to 30 seconds.' },
  { type: 'multi', q: 'Which of these retry until they pass or time out?', o: ['A web-first assertion on a locator, such as to_have_text', 'A web-first assertion on a page, such as to_have_url', 'locator.is_visible() read once', 'locator.count() read once', 'A Python assert on a string that was read earlier'], a: [0, 1], why: 'Assertions made with expect or assertThat retry. Values that you read first are a snapshot of one moment.' },
  { type: 'fill', q: L('Which Python assertion method checks that an element has exactly this text? (the method name)', 'Which Kotlin assertion method checks that an element has exactly this text? (the method name)'), ans: ['to_have_text', 'toHaveText', 'hasText'], why: 'It compares the whole text. to_contain_text (containsText) needs only a part.' },
  { q: 'You must check that a loading spinner is gone. What is right?', o: ['A web-first "hidden" assertion, which waits until it is gone', 'Read is_visible() once and compare', 'Sleep for 3 seconds and hope', 'Count the elements once'], a: 0, why: 'The opposite of a check retries as well. It passes when the spinner is gone, however long that takes (within the timeout).' },
  { q: 'One report is slow and needs 12 seconds. Where do you raise the timeout?', o: ['On that one assertion, with a timeout option', 'For the whole project, to 60 seconds', 'Nowhere, add a sleep', 'In the browser settings'], a: 0, why: 'A long timeout everywhere hides real problems. Be generous only where the slowness is expected.' }
],
practice: { id: 'p-m5', title: 'Assertion makeover',
  brief: '<p>Here is a flaky test for the TodoMVC demo. Rewrite it so that it is stable: no sleep, no plain assertions that read a value once, and with <b>web-first</b> assertions on the number of todos and on the text of the todo. Then run it on your machine, headed and slowed down, to check that it passes.</p>',
  template: { py: 'import time\n\n\ndef test_todo_added(page):\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?").fill("Buy milk")\n    page.keyboard.press("Enter")\n    time.sleep(2)\n    assert page.locator(".todo-list li").count() == 1\n    assert page.locator(".todo-list li label").inner_text() == "Buy milk"\n', kt: '@Test\nfun todoAdded() {\n    page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?").fill("Buy milk")\n    page.keyboard().press("Enter")\n    Thread.sleep(2000)\n    assertEquals(1, page.locator(".todo-list li").count())\n    assertEquals("Buy milk", page.locator(".todo-list li label").innerText())\n}\n' },
  checks: ['The sleep is gone', 'The count is checked with a count assertion on a locator', 'The text is checked with a text assertion on a locator', 'The locators use test ids or roles instead of CSS classes where possible', 'The test still passes when run headed with --slowmo'],
  model: code({ py: 'def test_todo_added(page: Page):\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    new_todo = page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?")\n    new_todo.fill("Buy milk")\n    new_todo.press("Enter")\n\n    expect(page.get_by_test_id("todo-item")).to_have_count(1)\n    expect(page.get_by_test_id("todo-title")).to_have_text("Buy milk")', kt: '@Test\nfun todoAdded() {\n    page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    val newTodo = page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")\n    newTodo.fill("Buy milk")\n    newTodo.press("Enter")\n\n    assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-item")).hasCount(1)\n    assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-title")).hasText("Buy milk")\n}', file: { py: 'Reference solution', kt: 'Reference solution' } }) + '<p>Four changes: the sleep is removed because the assertion waits; <code>count()</code> and <code>inner_text()</code> read once, so they became count and text assertions; the CSS selectors became test ids; and pressing Enter now happens on the input itself, so that the key goes where it should. The test is shorter, and it cannot fail because of timing.</p>' }
});

/* ===== Module 6 ===== */
COURSE.push({
id: 'm6', short: 'Structure and isolation', title: 'Structure and isolation: tests that stand on their own', minutes: 60,
lead: 'How to shape a test so that it is easy to read, why every test must be able to run alone, and how fixtures and lifecycle methods remove repeated setup.',
learn: ['shape a test as arrange, act, assert, with one reason to fail', 'explain why tests must not depend on each other', 'move repeated setup into a pytest fixture or a JUnit lifecycle method', 'run a single test or a tagged group of tests'],
body: `
<h2>Arrange, act, assert</h2>
<p>A good test reads like a short story with three parts. <b>Arrange</b>: get the world into the state the story needs (open the page, add two todos). <b>Act</b>: do the one thing the test is about (tick a todo). <b>Assert</b>: check what happened (one item is left). Keep the parts visible, even with a blank line between them. If a test has several "act and assert" rounds, it is usually two tests that want to be separated.</p>
<p>A test should have <b>one reason to fail</b>. When it fails, its name should tell you what broke, without reading the code: <code>completing_one_todo_leaves_one_item</code> is useful, <code>test3</code> is not.</p>
<h2>Every test stands on its own</h2>
<p>Imagine a restaurant where the second customer can only eat if the first one left a particular plate on the table. When the first customer does not come, nobody can eat, and nobody understands why. Tests that depend on each other behave the same way: one failure causes ten, they cannot run in parallel, and you cannot run just one of them.</p>
<p>Playwright helps: every test gets a fresh <b>browser context</b>, so no cookies, logins or saved data leak from one test to the next. The demo site saves todos in the browser, so you can see the isolation work:</p>
${code({ py: 'def test_a_adds_a_todo(page: Page):\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    new_todo = page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?")\n    new_todo.fill("Buy milk")\n    new_todo.press("Enter")\n    expect(page.get_by_test_id("todo-item")).to_have_count(1)\n\n\ndef test_b_starts_empty(page: Page):\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    expect(page.get_by_test_id("todo-item")).to_have_count(0)', kt: '@Test\nfun aAddsATodo() {\n    page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    val newTodo = page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")\n    newTodo.fill("Buy milk")\n    newTodo.press("Enter")\n    assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-item")).hasCount(1)\n}\n\n@Test\nfun bStartsEmpty() {\n    page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-item")).hasCount(0)\n}', file: { py: 'Isolation', kt: 'Isolation' } })}
<p>Test B passes although test A left a todo behind, because it never saw it: A and B each had their own context.</p>
<h2>Setup that many tests share</h2>
<p>When tests need the same starting point, you put the setup in one place. The idea is the same in both tracks, only the tool differs.</p>
${tbl(['', 'Python track (pytest)', 'Kotlin track (JUnit)'], [
  ['Shared setup', 'A <b>fixture</b>: a function marked <code>@pytest.fixture</code>. A test asks for it by naming it as a parameter', 'A method marked <code>@BeforeEach</code>, which runs before every test of the class'],
  ['Cleanup', 'Code after <code>yield</code> in the fixture', 'A method marked <code>@AfterEach</code>'],
  ['Once for many tests', 'A fixture with <code>scope="session"</code> or <code>"module"</code>', '<code>@BeforeAll</code> and <code>@AfterAll</code> (with <code>@TestInstance(PER_CLASS)</code>)'],
  ['Where the browser comes from', 'The pytest-playwright plugin gives you <code>page</code> and <code>browser</code>', 'Your <code>BaseTest</code> class from module 2'],
  ['Group and filter', '<code>@pytest.mark.smoke</code>, then <code>pytest -m smoke</code>', '<code>@Tag("smoke")</code>, then a Gradle tag filter']
])}
{{watch:s6}}
{{w:lifecycle}}
<h2>Small rules that keep a suite healthy</h2>
<ul><li><b>Do not share a page or a context</b> between tests, and do not keep them in global variables.</li><li><b>Create the data a test needs inside the test</b> (or in its fixture), not in the previous test.</li><li><b>Prefer short setups</b>. If the arrange part is longer than the rest, it may belong in a fixture or a helper.</li><li><b>Tag a few fast, important tests as smoke tests</b>, so that you can run them first on every change.</li></ul>
<h2>Step by step: your first fixture</h2>
${steps('m6', [
  { do: L('Create <code>tests/conftest.py</code> with a fixture that opens the page and adds two todos.', 'Create <code>TodoFixtureTest.kt</code>, whose <code>@BeforeEach</code> method opens the page and adds two todos.'), code: code({ py: 'import pytest\nfrom playwright.sync_api import Page\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef todo_page(page: Page) -> Page:\n    """A TodoMVC page that already has two todos."""\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    new_todo = page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?")\n    for title in ["Buy milk", "Walk the dog"]:\n        new_todo.fill(title)\n        new_todo.press("Enter")\n    return page', kt: 'package course\n\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.assertions.PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.options.AriaRole\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Tag\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test\n\nclass TodoFixtureTest : BaseTest() {\n    @BeforeEach\n    fun addTwoTodos() {\n        page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n        val newTodo = page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")\n        for (title in listOf("Buy milk", "Walk the dog")) {\n            newTodo.fill(title)\n            newTodo.press("Enter")\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    @Tag("smoke")\n    fun startsWithTwoItemsLeft() {\n        assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-count")).hasText("2 items left")\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun completingOneLeavesOne() {\n        page.getByTestId("todo-item").first().getByRole(AriaRole.CHECKBOX).check()\n        assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-count")).hasText("1 item left")\n    }\n}', file: { py: 'tests/conftest.py', kt: 'src/test/kotlin/course/TodoFixtureTest.kt' }, ex: 'fixture-setup' }), why: L('A file named <code>conftest.py</code> is read by pytest automatically, and its fixtures are available to every test next to it. The docstring says what the fixture gives you.', 'The <code>@BeforeEach</code> method of this class runs after the one in <code>BaseTest</code>. Superclass setup runs first, so the page already exists when your method uses it.'), see: 'Nothing yet.' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Create <code>tests/test_todo_fixture.py</code>, with two tests that ask for the fixture by name, and register the marker in <code>pytest.ini</code>.', code: code({ py: 'import pytest\nfrom playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\n@pytest.mark.smoke\ndef test_starts_with_two_items_left(todo_page: Page):\n    expect(todo_page.get_by_test_id("todo-count")).to_have_text("2 items left")\n\n\ndef test_completing_one_leaves_one(todo_page: Page):\n    todo_page.get_by_test_id("todo-item").first.get_by_role("checkbox").check()\n    expect(todo_page.get_by_test_id("todo-count")).to_have_text("1 item left")', file: { py: 'tests/test_todo_fixture.py' }, ex: 'fixture-tests' }), why: 'pytest sees the parameter name <code>todo_page</code>, finds the fixture with that name, runs it, and passes the result in. Add these lines to <code>pytest.ini</code> so that the marker is known: <code>markers =</code> and, indented below it, <code>smoke: quick checks that run on every change</code>.', see: 'Nothing yet.' },
  { do: 'Run the whole file, then only the smoke test.', code: sh({ py: 'pytest tests/test_todo_fixture.py\npytest -m smoke', kt: './gradlew test --tests "course.TodoFixtureTest"\n./gradlew test --tests "course.TodoFixtureTest.startsWithTwoItemsLeft"' }), why: 'Running one test alone is the proof that it does not depend on any other. Filtering by tag or by name is how you run a quick set while you work.', see: L('<code>2 passed</code>, then <code>1 passed, 1 deselected</code>', 'Both tests pass, then only <code>startsWithTwoItemsLeft</code> runs.') },
  { do: 'Prove the isolation: add the two tests from the "Every test stands on its own" section to your project and run them in both orders.', why: 'It is one thing to hear that contexts are separate, and another to see the second test start empty with your own eyes.', see: 'Both tests pass, in any order, although the first leaves a todo behind.' }
])}
`,
takeaways: ['Arrange, act, assert, with one reason to fail and a name that says what broke.', 'Tests must be able to run alone and in any order. Tests that depend on each other fail together.', 'A fresh browser context for every test is what keeps Playwright tests isolated.', 'Shared setup lives in a pytest fixture or in a JUnit @BeforeEach method. Cleanup goes after yield or in @AfterEach.', 'Tag a few quick tests as smoke tests and run them first.'],
quiz: [
  { q: 'What does AAA stand for in a test?', o: ['Arrange, act, assert', 'Always assert again', 'Automate, analyse, archive', 'Actions, assertions, attributes'], a: 0, why: 'Prepare the state, do the one thing under test, then check the result.' },
  { q: 'Which design is best?', o: ['Every test prepares its own data and can run alone, in any order', 'Test 2 uses the todo that test 1 created', 'All tests share one page that is opened once', 'One long test checks the whole application'], a: 0, why: 'Independent tests can be run alone, in parallel and in any order, and a failure points to a single cause.' },
  { q: 'Why do tests that depend on each other cause trouble?', o: ['One failure causes many, and they cannot run alone or in parallel', 'They run faster', 'Playwright forbids them', 'They use more memory'], a: 0, why: 'If test 1 fails, test 2 fails for a reason that has nothing to do with test 2, and you lose time looking at the wrong place.' },
  { q: L('What does the code after "yield" in a pytest fixture do?', 'What does a method marked @AfterEach do?'), o: [L('It runs after the test, as cleanup', 'It runs after every test, as cleanup'), L('It runs before the test', 'It runs once before all tests'), L('It runs only when the test fails', 'It runs only when a test fails'), L('It is ignored', 'It is ignored')], a: 0, why: L('Everything before yield is setup, and everything after it is teardown. The test runs at the yield.', 'It is the place for cleanup that must happen after each test, even when the test failed.') },
  { type: 'order', q: 'Put the parts of a good test in order.', items: ['Arrange: open the page and prepare the data', 'Act: do the one thing the test is about', 'Assert: check the result', 'Cleanup: usually done for you by the fixture or the lifecycle'], why: 'Arrange, act, assert, and then the framework tidies up.' },
  { type: 'fill', q: 'What do you get fresh for every test, so that cookies and saved data do not leak? (two words)', ans: ['browser context', 'context', 'a browser context'], why: 'A new context is cheap, and it starts with no cookies and no storage.' },
  { type: 'multi', q: 'Which of these are signs of a good test?', o: ['It checks one behaviour', 'Its name says what it checks', 'It passes or fails the same way whether other tests ran before or not', 'It checks twenty unrelated things in a row', 'It relies on data left by the previous test'], a: [0, 1, 2], why: 'Small, clearly named, independent tests are easy to read, to run and to trust.' }
],
practice: { id: 'p-m6', title: 'Fixtures and isolation on your machine',
  brief: '<p>In your project, create the setup that opens the TodoMVC demo and adds <b>three</b> todos ("Buy milk", "Walk the dog", "Write tests"). Write <b>three</b> tests that use it: (1) the counter says "3 items left"; (2) after ticking one todo the counter says "2 items left"; (3) after deleting "Walk the dog" (hover the row and click its Delete button) two rows remain. Tag the first as a smoke test. Run them all, then run the third alone.</p>',
  template: { py: '# tests/conftest.py\n\n\n# tests/test_todo_three.py\n', kt: '// TodoThreeTest.kt\n' },
  checks: ['The setup is in one place, not repeated in the tests', 'Each test can run alone and passes', 'The first test is tagged as smoke and runs with the marker filter', 'No test depends on the result of another', 'Each test has a name that says what it checks'],
  model: '<p>The fixture is the one from the step above, with a third title in the list. A possible third test:</p>' + code({ py: 'def test_deleting_one_leaves_two(todo_page: Page):\n    row = todo_page.get_by_test_id("todo-item").filter(has_text="Walk the dog")\n    row.hover()\n    row.get_by_role("button", name="Delete").click()\n    expect(todo_page.get_by_test_id("todo-item")).to_have_count(2)', kt: '@Test\nfun deletingOneLeavesTwo() {\n    val row = page.getByTestId("todo-item").filter(Locator.FilterOptions().setHasText("Walk the dog"))\n    row.hover()\n    row.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON, Locator.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Delete")).click()\n    assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-item")).hasCount(2)\n}', file: { py: 'Reference test', kt: 'Reference test' } }) + '<p>The hover is needed because the Delete button is only drawn while the mouse is over the row. Because every test gets its own context, deleting a todo here cannot disturb the other tests.</p>' }
});

SCENES.s7 = { id: 's7', title: 'from raw locators to a page object', run: false, intro: 'Three snapshots of the same test. Watch the knowledge of the page move out of the test and into a class. This is display only: it runs on your computer.', steps: [
  { full: true,
    py: 'def test_completes_and_clears(page: Page):\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?").fill("Buy milk")\n    page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?").press("Enter")\n    page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?").fill("Walk the dog")\n    page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?").press("Enter")\n    page.get_by_test_id("todo-item").filter(has_text="Buy milk").get_by_role("checkbox").check()\n    page.get_by_role("button", name="Clear completed").click()\n    expect(page.get_by_test_id("todo-item")).to_have_count(1)',
    kt: '@Test\nfun completesAndClears() {\n    page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?").fill("Buy milk")\n    page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?").press("Enter")\n    page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?").fill("Walk the dog")\n    page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?").press("Enter")\n    page.getByTestId("todo-item").filter(Locator.FilterOptions().setHasText("Buy milk")).getByRole(AriaRole.CHECKBOX).check()\n    page.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON, Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Clear completed")).click()\n    assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-item")).hasCount(1)\n}',
    say: 'A working test, but it knows too much about the page. The placeholder text appears four times, and the way to tick a todo is a long chain. If a developer changes the placeholder, every test that adds a todo must be edited.' },
  { full: true,
    py: 'class TodoPage:\n    URL = "https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/"\n\n    def __init__(self, page: Page):\n        self.page = page\n        self.new_todo = page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?")\n        self.items = page.get_by_test_id("todo-item")\n        self.clear_completed = page.get_by_role("button", name="Clear completed")\n\n    def open(self) -> None:\n        self.page.goto(self.URL)\n\n    def add(self, title: str) -> None:\n        self.new_todo.fill(title)\n        self.new_todo.press("Enter")\n\n    def complete(self, title: str) -> None:\n        self.items.filter(has_text=title).get_by_role("checkbox").check()',
    kt: 'class TodoPage(private val page: Page) {\n    val newTodo: Locator = page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")\n    val items: Locator = page.getByTestId("todo-item")\n    val clearCompleted: Locator = page.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON, Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Clear completed"))\n\n    fun open() {\n        page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n    }\n\n    fun add(title: String) {\n        newTodo.fill(title)\n        newTodo.press("Enter")\n    }\n\n    fun complete(title: String) {\n        items.filter(Locator.FilterOptions().setHasText(title)).getByRole(AriaRole.CHECKBOX).check()\n    }\n}',
    say: 'The page object is a class that <b>knows the page</b>. The locators are stored once, as properties. The actions have names from the business, like <code>add</code> and <code>complete</code>. Nothing here says what a test expects: that stays in the tests.' },
  { full: true,
    py: 'def test_completes_and_clears(page: Page):\n    todo = TodoPage(page)\n    todo.open()\n    todo.add("Buy milk")\n    todo.add("Walk the dog")\n\n    todo.complete("Buy milk")\n    todo.clear_completed.click()\n\n    expect(todo.items).to_have_count(1)',
    kt: '@Test\nfun completesAndClears() {\n    val todo = TodoPage(page)\n    todo.open()\n    todo.add("Buy milk")\n    todo.add("Walk the dog")\n\n    todo.complete("Buy milk")\n    todo.clearCompleted.click()\n\n    assertThat(todo.items).hasCount(1)\n}',
    say: 'The test now reads like the scenario a person would write: open, add two, complete one, clear, and check. If the placeholder changes tomorrow, you edit <b>one line</b> in <b>one file</b>, and no test changes.' }
] };

